  <h2 class="section-title">3 Foot Stroke · Gold Anodised Cylinder Barrels · Locomotive Scale · Bore TBD Phase 27</h2>
  <p class="section-lead">
    The Phase 26 pistons are a <strong style="color:var(--piston)">3 foot (914mm) stroke</strong> — the largest stroke in the Miasma series by a factor of approximately 3.7 versus the 250mm stroke in the Decker 9000. The cylinder barrels are <strong style="color:var(--piston)">gold anodised</strong> — structural chromoly 4340 with a hard anodised gold finish on the exterior barrel faces for visual identification and thermal signature.
    Bore is not yet confirmed — this is the primary Phase 27 calculation item. The torque table below uses bore estimates from 150mm through 250mm to bracket the expected output range. At 200mm bore the numbers are approximately: <strong style="color:var(--safe)">42,000 Nm at 90 PSI idle</strong> and <strong style="color:var(--safe)">234,000 Nm at 500 PSI maximum</strong>. These figures assume a cylinder count of two — same as the Decker 9000 — which may be increased in Phase 27 once the transmission design is confirmed capable of accepting the combined output.
    The 3 ft stroke piston is fundamentally a locomotive cylinder. Locomotive builders have used strokes in this range since the 1880s — the physics is proven. The engineering challenge is not the piston itself but the crankshaft geometry and the transmission that receives the output.
  </p>

