<div class="panel" id="panel-drive">
<div class="wrap">
  <div class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--new)">Diagram 4 · Dual Tank Belt Chain Drive — Phase 26</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Two ANSI #160 DS Chains in Parallel · Dual Bevel Gear Rear Turn · Split Torque · Tandem Output Shafts</h2>
  <p class="section-lead">
    The Berserker uses <strong style="color:var(--new)">two Tank Belt chain driveshafts running in parallel</strong> — side by side — from the transmission output to the rear bevel gear set. Each chain is ANSI #160 double strand — one step heavier than the Decker 9000's ANSI #120 DS — for a safe working load of approximately 24,000 lbs per chain (48,000 lbs combined). At the maximum estimated chain pull for the Berserker at 200mm bore 500 PSI, the combined safe load provides a healthy safety factor.
    The two chains run side by side in a single sealed housing with roller spacers top and bottom for both chains. They share the same oil bath. At the rear a <strong style="color:var(--new)">dual bevel gear assembly</strong> converts both chains' longitudinal rotation 90 degrees into the transverse rear axle direction simultaneously — both chains drive the same bevel gear input pinion shaft via two sprockets on that shaft, combining their torque before the 90 degree turn.
    Failure mode advantage over single chain: if one chain fails under extreme load the second chain continues to carry full drive — the vehicle does not immediately lose drive, it loses half its maximum torque which at 500 PSI still leaves approximately 117,000 Nm at the wheels — enough to complete the journey and reach a service point.
  </p>

