  <div class="warn-box">
    <h4>⚠ IMPORTANT CONTEXT ON THE HORSEPOWER NUMBERS</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>The HP figures above are instantaneous peak figures at full steam pressure — they assume the boiler can sustain steam delivery at that pressure for the duration of the power stroke, which at 100 gallon multi-bowl scale is the design intent to confirm in Phase 27</li>
      <li>At 900 RPM and 914mm stroke the piston mean speed is ~27.4 m/s — this is higher than traditional locomotive piston speeds and Phase 27 must confirm the liner, rings, and lubrication system can sustain this — if RPM is capped lower the HP figures reduce proportionally</li>
      <li>Practical target for Phase 26 first build is 90–250 PSI at 600–750 RPM — the 3,100 HP reference in the banner is based on this working range, not the theoretical 500 PSI maximum</li>
      <li>At 90 PSI idle the torque of 42,000 Nm already exceeds the rated torque of the strongest production diesel trucks (Mack Titan: ~5,900 Nm) by approximately 7× — even at idle this engine is not subtle</li>
      <li>The transmission must handle the idle torque of 42,000 Nm — this is the design constraint, not the peak — any transmission that survives idle can be protected against peak by the PID pressure limiter</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

